Scenario ID: S05
Scenario title: Specification Document Review
Initial workflow description:
An engineer reviews a specification document to check whether new features are reflected correctly and whether there are typos or missing details.

Domain expert answers:
{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "What event or signal starts the review — for example, does the engineer receive an automated notification, a manual assignment in a task system, or an email — and where does the specification document come from at that moment?",
      "answer": "The specific trigger mechanism is not specified. What is defined is that the reviewer collects the specification document and a related feature change list from a document repository as the first activity. How the reviewer is notified or assigned is not detailed in the process."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "What specific criteria or checklist does the engineer use to decide whether a new feature is correctly reflected in the document?",
      "answer": "The reviewer uses a review checklist as an input and compares the feature change list against the document content. The key decision criterion is whether all feature changes are reflected in the document. If any are missing, revisions are requested. Specific checklist items beyond feature coverage and terminology consistency are not detailed."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "Can you walk me through the sequence of individual steps the engineer performs during the review, from opening the document to completing it?",
      "answer": "The reviewer: (1) collects the specification document and feature change list; (2) compares feature changes with document content; (3) checks terminology and formatting consistency; (4) records comments and requested revisions. After that, the document author revises the document, a feature owner confirms revisions, and an approver gives final approval. Steps 2–4 are the reviewer's core activities."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "What are the possible outcomes at the end of the review, and what action or next step does each outcome trigger?",
      "answer": "Two decisions exist. First: if all feature changes are reflected, the review proceeds; if not, revisions are requested. Second: after the author revises the document, if revisions are acceptable the approver approves the final document; if not, another revision cycle is requested. Outputs include review comments, a change request list, an approval decision, and an updated document."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "Which tools or systems does the engineer use during the review, and in what file format is the specification document typically stored?",
      "answer": "The reviewer uses a document repository, a change request tracker, a review checklist, and a commenting tool. The file format of the specification document is not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "After the engineer completes the review, who receives the document or the review findings, and does any other role participate in the process before or after the engineer's review?",
      "answer": "After the reviewer records comments, the document author receives them and revises the document. The feature owner then confirms the revisions. Finally, an approver gives the approval decision. So three other roles participate after the reviewer: document author, feature owner, and approver. No roles are specified as acting before the reviewer."
    }
  ]
}
